# 🤖 LLM + ASIC — Reasoning and Enforcement Separation

### A Hardware-Rooted AI Security Architecture Pattern

> **Concepts document — part of the ScryptGate research framework**  
> *An architectural pattern for giving standard LLMs access to dedicated  
> cryptographic hardware as callable security tools — separating AI reasoning  
> from hardware enforcement.*

-----

> **Prior Art Notice:** No existing published work on using cryptographic  
> mining ASICs as callable LLM security tools was found at time of writing.  
> This document establishes that record. First published: **March 22, 2026.**

-----

## The Core Idea

The most powerful configuration is not running an LLM on hashing silicon.

It is giving a standard LLM **access** to hashing silicon as a callable tool.

```
Current AI security model:
    LLM reasons about security
            ↓
    Software enforces the decision
            ↓
    Software can be bypassed, spoofed, or compromised

This architecture:
    LLM reasons about security
            ↓
    Hardware enforces the decision
            ↓
    Hardware cannot be bypassed by software — ever
```

The LLM provides reasoning. The ASIC provides enforcement. Neither can do the other’s job. Together they produce **hardware-rooted AI security decisions** — a class of guarantee that software-only AI architectures architecturally cannot provide regardless of how well they are written.

