## The Separation Principle

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 REASONING LAYER                  │
│                                                  │
│   LLM (any model, any size, any architecture)   │
│   LangChain / agent orchestration               │
│   Context, history, threat intelligence         │
│   Human-in-the-loop approval                    │
│                                                  │
│   "Should this action be allowed?"              │
│   "Does this model look legitimate?"            │
│   "Is this traffic pattern anomalous?"          │
└──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┘
                       │ tool call
                       ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│               ENFORCEMENT LAYER                  │
│                                                  │
│   Cryptographic ASIC (any algorithm)            │
│   Hardware proof-of-work verification           │
│   Hardware model integrity hashing              │
│   Hardware rate limiting                        │
│                                                  │
│   "The answer is verified in silicon"           │
│   "No software can override this result"        │
│   "Fails closed if hardware unreachable"        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The boundary between these two layers is the architectural innovation. Crossing that boundary with a tool call transforms a software security decision into a hardware-enforced one.

