## The Four Hardware Tools

### Tool 1 — verify_request_pow

```
Purpose:    Gate all agent action requests behind hardware PoW
When used:  Before queuing any proposal for human review
What it does:
    → ASIC verifies Scrypt PoW at 185M/s
    → Software cannot fake a valid token faster
    → Invalid token = request rejected before LLM even sees it
    → Valid token = LLM reasons about whether to approve

LLM role:   "Should I approve this action?"
ASIC role:  "Did this request actually do the work to get here?"
```

### Tool 2 — verify_model_integrity

```
Purpose:    Verify model weights before loading into inference
When used:  Every time a model is loaded — no exceptions
What it does:
    → ASIC streams and hashes model weights
    → Hash compared against vault-stored reference
    → Vault is air-gapped — no network interface ever
    → Mismatch = model does not load

LLM role:   "Should I load this model for this task?"
ASIC role:  "Are these weights what they claim to be?"
```

### Tool 3 — get_hardware_entropy

```
Purpose:    Hardware-quality randomness for cryptographic operations
When used:  Key generation, nonce creation, token generation
What it does:
    → ASIC generates entropy from hardware hashing process
    → Not software PRNG — actual hardware entropy source
    → Software cannot predict or influence the output

LLM role:   "I need a nonce for this operation"
ASIC role:  "Here is entropy no software predicted"
```

### Tool 4 — hardware_rate_check

```
Purpose:    Rate limiting that software cannot reset or bypass
When used:  Before processing repeated requests from same source
What it does:
    → Counters tracked in ASIC memory
    → Cannot be reset by compromising the software stack
    → Only resets on hardware power cycle

LLM role:   "Is this source making too many requests?"
ASIC role:  "Here is a count no software can falsify"
```

