## Why Hardware Enforcement Changes Everything

### The Software Security Ceiling

Every software security system has the same fundamental weakness:

```
Security check lives in software
    → Runs in a process
    → Process has a memory space
    → Memory space can be read/written by privileged code
    → Privileged code can modify or bypass the check
    → Therefore: no software security check is absolute
```

This is not a flaw in any specific implementation. It is a property of software running on general-purpose hardware.

### What Hardware Provides

```
Security check lives in silicon
    → Runs on dedicated hardware
    → Hardware has no general-purpose memory accessible to software
    → No software process can modify what silicon decided
    → Therefore: hardware security checks are absolute
    → (Subject only to physical access attacks)
```

### The Combined Property

When an LLM calls a hardware tool:

```
LLM provides:     Context, reasoning, nuance, adaptability
                  "This LOOKS suspicious because..."
                  "Given the history of this source..."
                  "The risk level appears to be..."

Hardware provides: Absolute enforcement, unfakeability
                  "The PoW is valid or it is not"
                  "The hash matches or it does not"
                  "The rate limit is exceeded or it is not"
```

Neither layer alone is sufficient:

- Hardware alone: enforces blindly, no reasoning about context
- LLM alone: reasons well, enforces in software — bypassable

Together: reasons about context, enforces in hardware — the ceiling of achievable security without dedicated HSMs costing thousands of dollars.

